Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. The term involving only $y$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=z\cdot 2 x+0\\&=2 x z
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $z$, holding $x$ and $y$ fixed. The term involving only $x$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}&=- 3 y^{2}\cdot 1+0\\&=- 3 y^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. The term involving only $z$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- x^{3}\cdot 1+0\\&=- x^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $x$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\left(y + z\right)\cos x+2 \cos{\left(z \right)}\cdot 1\\&=\left(y + z\right) \cos{\left(x \right)} + 2 \cos{\left(z \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $z$, holding $x$ and $y$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $z$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}&=- 3 \sin{\left(y \right)}\cdot 1+\left(2 x + 2 y\right)\left(-\sin z\right)\\&=\left(- 2 x - 2 y\right) \sin{\left(z \right)} - 3 \sin{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- \sin{\left(x \right)}\cdot 1+\left(2 x + 2 z\right)\left(-\sin y\right)\\&=\left(- 2 x - 2 z\right) \sin{\left(y \right)} - \sin{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $z$, holding $x$ and $y$ fixed. Use the constant multiple and chain rules, then substitute the coordinates. At this point the radicand is $25>0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}&=x\cdot\frac{1}{2\sqrt{y^{2} + 4 z^{2}}}\cdot 8 z\\&=\frac{4 x z}{\sqrt{y^{2} + 4 z^{2}}}\\\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\big|_{(1,4,\frac{3}{2})}&=\frac{4\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)(1)}{\sqrt{25}}\\&=\frac{6}{5}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Use the constant multiple and chain rules, then substitute the coordinates. At this point the radicand is $25>0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=z\cdot\frac{1}{2\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\cdot 8 x\\&=\frac{4 x z}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\big|_{(- \frac{3}{2},4,2)}&=\frac{4\left(- \frac{3}{2}\right)(2)}{\sqrt{25}}\\&=- \frac{12}{5}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Use the constant multiple and chain rules, then substitute the coordinates. At this point the radicand is $25>0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=z\cdot\frac{1}{2\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\cdot 8 x\\&=\frac{4 x z}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\big|_{(- \frac{3}{2},4,2)}&=\frac{4\left(- \frac{3}{2}\right)(2)}{\sqrt{25}}\\&=- \frac{12}{5}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=- x y^{3}\\
u&=x^{2},\quad v=\cos{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_x&=2 x\\
v_x&=- \sin{\left(x \right)}\\
r_x&=- y^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\
&=1\left[\left(2 x\right)\left(\cos{\left(x \right)}\right)+\left(x^{2}\right)\left(- \sin{\left(x \right)}\right)\right]+\left(- y^{3}\right)\\
&=- x^{2} \sin{\left(x \right)} + 2 x \cos{\left(x \right)} - y^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=x,\quad r=- x^{2}\\
u&=y^{2},\quad v=\sin{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=2 y\\
v_y&=\cos{\left(y \right)}\\
r_y&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=x\left[\left(2 y\right)\left(\sin{\left(y \right)}\right)+\left(y^{2}\right)\left(\cos{\left(y \right)}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=x \left(y^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} + 2 y \sin{\left(y \right)}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=x^{3},\quad r=0\\
u&=y^{4},\quad v=e^{y^{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=4 y^{3}\\
v_y&=3 y^{2} e^{y^{3}}\\
r_y&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=x^{3}\left[\left(4 y^{3}\right)\left(e^{y^{3}}\right)+\left(y^{4}\right)\left(3 y^{2} e^{y^{3}}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=x^{3} \left(3 y^{6} e^{y^{3}} + 4 y^{3} e^{y^{3}}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=x^{2} y^{2}\\
u&=x,\quad v=e^{x y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_x&=1\\
v_x&=y e^{x y}\\
r_x&=2 x y^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\
&=1\left[\left(1\right)\left(e^{x y}\right)+\left(x\right)\left(y e^{x y}\right)\right]+\left(2 x y^{2}\right)\\
&=2 x y^{2} + x y e^{x y} + e^{x y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(1,2)&=\left(1\right)\left(e^{2}\right)+\left(1\right)\left(2 e^{2}\right)+\left(8\right)\\
&=8 + 3 e^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=x,\quad r=- \sin{\left(y \right)}\\
u&=y,\quad v=\cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=1\\
v_y&=- \sin{\left(y \right)}\\
r_y&=- \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=x\left[\left(1\right)\left(\cos{\left(y \right)}\right)+\left(y\right)\left(- \sin{\left(y \right)}\right)\right]+\left(- \cos{\left(y \right)}\right)\\
&=x \left(- y \sin{\left(y \right)} + \cos{\left(y \right)}\right) - \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=2$ and $y=\pi$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y(2,\pi)&=2\left[\left(1\right)\left(-1\right)+\left(\pi\right)\left(0\right)\right]+\left(1\right)\\
&=-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=y,\quad v=\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=1\\
v_y&=\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\
r_y&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=1\left[\left(1\right)\left(\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\right)+\left(y\right)\left(\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=\frac{y^{2}}{\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} + \sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=3$ and $y=4$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y(3,4)&=\left(1\right)\left(5\right)+\left(4\right)\left(\frac{4}{5}\right)\\
&=\frac{41}{5}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=x + y^{2},\quad v=y + \ln{\left(x y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=2 y\\
v_y&=1 + \frac{1}{y}\\
r_y&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=2 y \left(y + \ln{\left(x y \right)}\right)\\
&\quad +\left(\left(1 + \frac{1}{y}\right) \left(x + y^{2}\right)\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=1$ and $y=1$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y(1,1)&=\left(2\right)\left(1\right)+\left(2\right)\left(2\right)\\
&=6
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=x^{2} + y,\quad v=e^{x y} + \ln{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_x&=2 x\\
v_x&=y e^{x y} + \frac{1}{x}\\
r_x&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\
&=2 x \left(e^{x y} + \ln{\left(x \right)}\right)\\
&\quad +\left(\left(x^{2} + y\right) \left(y e^{x y} + \frac{1}{x}\right)\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=1$ and $y=1$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(1,1)&=\left(2\right)\left(e\right)+\left(2\right)\left(1 + e\right)\\
&=2 + 4 e
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=x^{2} + y,\quad v=- x + \ln{\left(x y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_x&=2 x\\
v_x&=-1 + \frac{1}{x}\\
r_x&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\
&=2 x \left(- x + \ln{\left(x y \right)}\right)\\
&\quad +\left(\left(-1 + \frac{1}{x}\right) \left(x^{2} + y\right)\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(2,1)&=\left(4\right)\left(-2 + \ln{\left(2 \right)}\right)+\left(5\right)\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)\\
&=- \frac{21}{2} + 4 \ln{\left(2 \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
